    <body>
      <sec id="article-51595.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Left ventricular pseudoaneurysms (LVP) are a very rare but lethal complication of myocardial infarction, cardiac surgery, trauma, and infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;LVP is characterized&#x000a0;by left ventricular wall ruptures and is contained by the pericardium or scar tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with LVP can&#x000a0;present with congestive heart failure and arrhythmias. The development of tamponade after left ventricular rupture&#x000a0;is usually&#x000a0;fatal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Casapo et al reported an incidence&#x000a0;rate of LVP of approximately 0.29% (Clin Cardiol 1997). Studies suggest that, of all causes of LVP, myocardial infarction and cardiac surgery result in 55% and 33%, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;In cardiac surgery, Mitral Valve replacement is associated with a higher rate of LVP. <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r5">[5]</xref>Traditionally, the preferred management of a left ventricular pseudoaneurysm has been emergency surgical intervention. The surgery itself has a high mortality ranging from 20% to 36%, but some of the recent studies&#x000a0;have shown mortality reduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r1">[1]</xref>[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;When surgery&#x000a0;is contraindicated because of high risk and medical management is&#x000a0;employed, mortality is&#x000a0;close to 48 %.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;But over the last 2 decades, advances in transcatheter procedural approaches and technology have made percutaneous closure of LVP an increasingly practical option, especially for patients with an unfavorable predicted surgical outcome. Percutaneous closure of the left ventricular pseudoaneurysm was first described by Clift et al in 2004.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;In 2016, Okuyama and colleagues reported the first case of a PTC successfully performed following transcatheter aortic valve replacement using&#x000a0;transapical access.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Before that, most cases reported in the literature were performed via surgery following life-threatening myocardial infarctions. There is no large study on the transcatheter management of LVP, but multiple successful&#x000a0;case studies have been reported, which&#x000a0;demonstrate its potential. There are also reports of a hybrid approach that&#x000a0;uses the PTC of the LVP to first stabilize the patient in the emergency setting, followed by surgical closure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;This article will discuss the anatomy of a left ventricular pseudoaneurysm, potential etiologies of LVP, indications for PTC, contraindications for PTC, the technique of PTC, possible complications of PTC, and the clinical significance of the advent of&#x000a0;the PTC procedure.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s2" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>The left ventricle is made up of a dense muscle and forms the thickest muscle of the heart. Pseudoaneurysm of the left ventricle is more common than a true aneurysm. A pseudoaneurysm&#x000a0;occurs when the ventricular wall&#x000a0;ruptures, contained only by the pericardium,&#x000a0;whereas a full-thickness, noncontractile, bulging portion of the ventricular wall characterizes a true aneurysm. Left ventricular pseudoaneurysm requires immediate intervention with either surgery or minimally invasive percutaneous transcatheter closure.</p>
        <p>According to a study by Yeo et al., there is an association between the location of a pseudoaneurysm and its etiology. For example, following myocardial infarction, 82% of&#x000a0;pseudoaneurysms&#x000a0;are located in the inferior or posterolateral wall. Following congenital&#x000a0;cardiac surgery, 87% of pseudoaneurysms are found in the right ventricular outflow tract. After mitral valve replacement, 100% of pseudoaneurysms&#x000a0;are located in the posterior subannular region of the mitral valve, and following aortic valve replacement, all pseudoaneurysms&#x000a0;are located in the subaortic region.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s3" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Currently, the preferred&#x000a0;treatment approach for LVP is emergency surgical intervention. However, percutaneous transcatheter LV closure is a feasible alternative for&#x000a0;persons at high risk for open surgery, including those with significant comorbidities and previous cardiac surgery, and those presenting with left ventricular pseudoaneurysm following a cardiac intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;A smaller pseudoaneurysm may be more readily addressed via the transcatheter approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s4" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Contraindications to percutaneous transcatheter closure include left atrial thrombus due to risk for embolism,&#x000a0;active endocarditis, and/or the presence of&#x000a0;cardiac anatomy unfavorable to catheter intervention, such as a large aneurysm neck or severely damaged tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s5" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Catheter equipment for closure of a left ventricular pseudoaneurysm includes vessel occluders and plugs, delivery and diagnostic catheters and sheaths, 0.035 or 0.038 inch guidewires, embolization coils, and microcatheters for delivery, and a transesophageal echocardiography apparatus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s6" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>A skilled interventional cardiologist or structuralist knowledgeable or trained in percutaneous transcatheter LV closure technique is required to successfully and safely complete the procedure. Operator experience and volume are important factors, but given the rarity of this condition, even large referral centers have limited experience.&#x000a0;Additional personnel include a cardiac nurse, a catheterization lab technician, a cardiothoracic surgeon, and a cardiac anesthesiologist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s7" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Percutaneous transcatheter left ventricular pseudoaneurysm closure is usually an emergent intervention. If scheduled, the patient should fast for 6 to 8 hours before the procedure, and anticoagulation should be held to normalize the international normalized ratio (INR) to at least 1.7. A preoperative anesthesiology assessment is important.&#x000a0;Before performing the PTC, right- and/or left-heart catheterization&#x000a0;is performed to assess pressures within the heart and lungs. Additionally, a left ventriculogram is performed to assess the function and structure of the left ventricular wall. Pre-procedure cardiac imaging provides vital&#x000a0;information to&#x000a0;enable detailed planning of an approach to address the anatomical variations of a left ventricular pseudoaneurysm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Detailed anatomy of the pseudo-aneurysmal&#x000a0;sac and its communication can be easily&#x000a0;viewed and assessed using a transoesophageal echocardiogram, CT Angiogram, or cardiac MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s8" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Under transesophageal echocardiogram monitoring and anesthesia, a femoral vein approach is used to access the left atrium and enter the left ventricle.&#x000a0;Approach options include retrograde access through the femoral artery and aorta to reach the left ventricle. A 7 French AL-2 (Amplatz Left Middle Type) guide catheter is used to&#x000a0;access the pseudoaneurysm, and a 0.035-in glide wire is advanced into it. A 5 Fr hypo tube is threaded over the glide wire into the cavity of the aneurysm, and the glide wire is removed.&#x000a0;Multiple embolization coils of different lengths are advanced into the aneurysm cavity. The neck of the pseudoaneurysm is then occluded using a 12 mm AGA Amplatzer II vascular plug or duct occluder. There should be no communication between the LV cavity and the pseudoaneurysm cavity after deploying the plug, as confirmed by angiography and transesophageal echocardiography. Following completion of the procedure, the patient should be monitored in the hospital for 1 to 2 days to assess for any complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Regular echocardiographic monitoring is the recommended post-op procedure.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s9" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of a percutaneous transcatheter left ventricular pseudoaneurysm closure include, but are not limited to, the following: bleeding, hematoma, pericardial effusion or tamponade, infection, embolism of the&#x000a0;closure device, arrhythmia, pacemaker dependence, stroke, and/or death. The transcatheter approach is&#x000a0;relatively new, and the&#x000a0;incidence of LVP is low; additional studies are needed to determine&#x000a0;the possible long-term effects/complications of this procedure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s10" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The PTC approach to a left ventricular pseudoaneurysm can provide lifesaving treatment to a cohort of high-risk patients for whom both open surgery and no intervention carry a very high mortality. PTC intervention carries a lower risk than the need for sternotomy and cardiopulmonary bypass. Catheter management of a left ventricular pseudoaneurysm can help prevent extension of the ventricular rupture and can serve as a bridge to open surgery in a subset of patients.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-51595.s11" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The management of left ventricular wall pseudoaneurysm is challenging and complex, requiring significant interdisciplinary collaboration. While once the domain of the cardiac surgeon, percutaneous&#x000a0;transcatheter closure of the left ventricular pseudoaneurysm may be&#x000a0;an alternative for patients who cannot tolerate surgical intervention. The less invasive procedure reduces the length of hospital stay and possible complications from sternotomy and cardiopulmonary bypass.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;To optimize outcomes, the goals and objectives of the percutaneous transcatheter LVP closure&#x000a0;are defined before&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;procedure. Infection must be treated before intervention, requiring the assistance of an infectious disease specialist.&#x000a0;If possible,&#x000a0;a comprehensive preoperative&#x000a0;evaluation should be&#x000a0;conducted, including&#x000a0;consultation with a pulmonologist and cardiologist. Because of the potential risk of embolism, an echocardiogram is performed&#x000a0;before intervention to rule out the presence of a left atrial appendage thrombus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r20">[20]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold></p>
        <p>An interprofessional team involving the specialty-trained clinicians and nurses is crucial in the preoperative, interoperative, and postoperative settings. Specialists include radiologists, pharmacologists, pulmonologists, cardiologists, interventional cardiologists, cardiothoracic surgeons, internal medicine and critical care specialists.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;In the postoperative period, the roles of the nurse and the pharmacist are crucial. The patients must be monitored for pain, wound infection, hematoma or hemorrhage, and a variety of common postoperative complications such as atelectasis, deep vein thrombosis, and pain. Pharmacist involvement is essential for parenteral nutrition if complications result. Clinical outcome of percutaneous transcatheter left ventricular pseudoaneurysm closure depends on the&#x000a0;extent and&#x000a0;location of the pseudoaneurysm, and the skill of the operator.&#x000a0;Optimal results also rely on the&#x000a0;interprofessional&#x000a0;team and timely communication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-51595.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
